course: dictionary
lesson: 15667
title: Using "those"
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Those» is the plural of «that». Use it for more than one thing or person that is farther away from you: «Look at those birds on the far hill.» If the birds were close to you, you would say «these» instead.
explain: «Those» can also point back to things already talked about, so you do not have to repeat them: «We picked apples and pears. Those apples were sweet.» Here «those» means the apples we already mentioned.
explain: Often «those» comes before a noun, as in «those days» or «those apples». Sometimes it stands alone, as in «those who» or «one of those». Look at the words after it to see how it is being used.
fact: «Those» is the plural of «that».
fact: Use «those» for things or people farther away, and «these» for things close to you.
fact: «Those» can point back to something already mentioned, such as «those apples».
fact: «Those who» means the people who: «Those who arrive early get the best seats.»
fact: «One of those» means one thing from a group like the one just described: «It was one of those days.»
exercise: «Look at those ducks on the far side of the lake.» Which word points to ducks that are far away? | that | these | this | *those
exercise: «We bought some pears. Those were ripe.» What does «those» refer to? | *the pears | the bag | the day | the shop
exercise: Which phrase means the people who? | these who | *those who | that who | this who
test: Which word is the plural of «that»? | these | this | *those | them
test: Which word would you use for three shoes across the room? | *those | it | these | this
exam: Which sentence uses «those» correctly? | *Look at those ducks on the far side of the lake. | I have those yesterday at home. | Those is my favourite colour and I like it. | Those want to go home now, ducks.
